  <text>
    <body>
      <div type="clause" n="8.4">
        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:Duns">Duns</ref> 8.4</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="8.4" corresp="#wit-b"><pb n="378" ed="#liebermann"/>Gelice ðam<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="6" target="#Duns_8-4.lnote.oe.6"/> Ænglisc sceal Wyliscan riht<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="17" target="#Duns_8-4.lnote.oe.17"/> wyrcean.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="8.4" corresp="#wit-q"><pb n="379" ed="#liebermann"/>Similiter debet Anglico Waliscus rectum facere.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="de" source="#liebermann"><pb n="379" ed="#liebermann"/>Diesem gleich soll der<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#Duns_8-4.lnote.de.1"/> Engländer dem Wälschen Recht erfüllen.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude" ana="#fromGerman">In the same way the Englishman shall do justice to the Welshman.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#unattributed" corresp="#wit-b">Similarly, the Englishman should work to implement the rights of the Welshman.</ab>
        <note type="editorial">U: translator unrecorded: check; The Quadripartitus reverses this: ‘Similiter debet Anglico Waliscus rectum facere’ (the Welshman to the Englishman).</note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Duns_8-4.lnote.oe.6" n="6" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">378</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">ðæm <hi rend="italic">Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">ðæm Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Duns_8-4.lnote.oe.17" n="17" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">378</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">-te <hi rend="italic">Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">-te Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Duns_8-4.lnote.de.1" n="1" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">219</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Q (danach Schmid) dreht das Subjekt unrichtig um; denn das Gesetz 8, 3 meinte mit ‘jenseits’ einen Walliser Beklagten.—Der Artikel fehlt archaisch</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Q (and Schmid after him) wrongly reverses the subject; for the law 8, 3 meant by ‘across the river’ a Welsh defendant.—The article is absent, an archaism</seg></note>
      </div>
    </body>
